REAL DEFECT — reproduced live in a real browser (Playwright/Chromium),
confirmed in-scope and newly introduced by this diff:

Clicking "+ Add Person" inserts a person row whose birth-month input is
`required` (whatif-rate-assumptions.js addPersonRow, this diff). That input
lives inside the SAME large <form> (rate-assumptions.html:7-399) as
~30 unrelated fields (tax_deferred_percent, filing_status, dividend yield,
cost basis, ACA fields, allocations, etc.), all covered by that form's own
`hx-trigger="change delay:500ms, input delay:500ms from:find
input[type=number]"`. Because base.js sets
`htmx.config.reportValidityOfForms = true` (confirmed already present in
this task's BASE, i.e. WS4's fix, not part of this diff), htmx validates
EVERY control in the form before sending ANY request from it — including
controls the user never touched.

Verified with two Playwright runs against a temp server built from my
snapshot (BUDGET_LISTEN_ADDR=127.0.0.1:44061, cp -rL data, both processes
killed after):
- Control (no Add Person): editing #tax-deferred-percent-input to 57 and
  blurring → POST /whatif/settings fires, 200, reload shows 57 persisted.
- Repro (Add Person clicked first, its birth-month field left blank, then
  the SAME edit to an unrelated, fully valid field): NO POST
  /whatif/settings is ever sent. `document.activeElement` after the
  attempt is the new row's `person_birth_month[]` input (focus silently
  stolen away from the field the user was editing), and a native "Please
  fill out this field" tooltip appears next to the Persons section, not
  near Rate Assumptions where the user was working (screenshot:
  probe2-after.png). No `role="alert"` element appears anywhere — the
  failure is otherwise silent. Reload confirms the tax_deferred_percent
  edit was NOT saved.

This blocking did not exist before this attempt: `grep required` on the
task's BASE (fix/whatif-save-path-2, via `git archive`, read-only) returns
zero matches in rate-assumptions.html and the JS — WS5 is the only diff
that adds `required` anywhere in this form. So this is not a WS4-class
pre-existing gap; it's a new interaction opened by this attempt's specific
implementation choice (client-side `required` on a field living inside a
shared multi-field autosave form, with no scoping of the validation and no
feedback near the field the user actually edited).

This is the exact probe named in my dispatch ("does it now block unrelated
saves on that form until the row is filled — is that acceptable per
D7/WS4?"). Judgment: not acceptable. It contradicts this run's own
governing principle for this exact form (D1/WS1: "an untouched input never
changes the saved value and never blocks the form") — here even a fully
valid, directly-edited field silently fails to save because of a sibling
row's incomplete state, with zero visible feedback near the edit itself.
It also reopens the WS4 failure mode ("Request errors are visible and
never wipe input") for a class of error WS4 didn't anticipate (client-side
validation blocking a DIFFERENT field's save). The fix is achievable
inside WS5's own territory (e.g., a targeted server-side refusal for the
name-only row, mirroring personRemovalBlockedByHealthcare, instead of a
form-wide client `required`; or scoping validation/reporting to the row
being edited) without reintroducing the 500 D7 is meant to close.

No other defect found under the enumerated attack surfaces: glide path
MCP/scenario-copy paths are N/A (GlidePath only referenced in
models/handlers.go/handlers_rates.go, no MCP write path touches it);
keyboard (Space) toggling is identical to mouse for onchange, no separate
path; edit-then-untick on an enabled glide path discards the edits and
disables using the prior saved values (safe, matches D6); refusal-while-
other-fields-changed on the settings form drops the WHOLE request
("nothing saved" is the literal, spec-conforming reading, confirmed by the
same request-boundary the code already enforces); personRemovalBlockedByHealthcare
compares by ID not name; removing the primary person is not newly affected
(parsePersonsForm's lack of a primary-required check is pre-existing,
untouched by this diff — flagged as a backlog OBSERVATION, not a FAIL).
